From ee6d1e134fc3b1e8f0506ed321056f11fbfd4284 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 01:13:02 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] JIT: do not commit the RSP increment when `pop r/m`
 faults
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

x86 requires the stack pointer to be unchanged when an instruction faults.
`pop r/m` (opcode 0x8F) was dispatched through the fused IR `Pop`, which the
AArch64 JIT emits as a single post-indexed load (`ldr<IndexType::POST>`): the
RSP increment is committed by the same operation that loads the value, before
the store to the destination. If the destination is unmapped, the store faults
after RSP has already been advanced, so the signal frame handed to a guest
handler carried the already-incremented stack pointer.

That is the Wine ntdll:exception `segfault_code` sequence — `xor eax,eax ;
popq (%rax) ; ret` with the handler resuming at `context->Rip += 2`. The handler
read the wrong RSP, the resume re-faulted, and the test died before producing any
output.

For a memory destination, split the sequence: load the value from [RSP] without
the increment, store it (the only faultable step), and update the stack pointer
only afterwards, so a fault leaves RSP untouched. Register destinations cannot
fault, so they keep the cheaper fused path.

tests: `tests/pop-fault` (differential against `mov %eax,(%rax)`; PASS after,
FAIL before).
---
 .../Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp       | 25 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp
index 0f7a00eee..80d308312 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.cpp
@@ -396,8 +396,31 @@ void OpDispatchBuilder::PUSHSegmentOp(OpcodeArgs, uint32_t SegmentReg) {
 }
 
 void OpDispatchBuilder::POPOp(OpcodeArgs) {
-  Ref Value = Pop(OpSizeFromSrc(Op));
+  const auto Size = OpSizeFromSrc(Op);
+
+  if (Op->Dest.IsGPR()) {
+    // Register destination: the store cannot fault, so the fused post-index
+    // `_Pop` (which updates RSP in the same operation) is safe and cheapest.
+    Ref Value = Pop(Size);
+    StoreResultGPR(Op, Value);
+    return;
+  }
+
+  // Memory destination. x86 leaves RSP unchanged if an instruction faults, and
+  // the store to memory is the only faultable part of `pop r/m`. The stack
+  // pointer increment must therefore be committed *after* the store, not folded
+  // into the address RMW.
+  Ref SP = LoadGPRRegister(X86State::REG_RSP);
+
+  // Load the value from [RSP] without advancing the stack pointer. The address
+  // is scratch, so the load leaves State.rip/RSP untouched if it faults.
+  auto Value = _LoadMem(RegClass::GPR, Size, SP, Invalid(), OpSize::i8Bit, MemOffsetType::SXTX, 1);
+
+  // Faultable store of the popped value to the destination.
   StoreResultGPR(Op, Value);
+
+  // Only now commit the stack pointer increment.
+  StoreGPRRegister(X86State::REG_RSP, Add(OpSize::i64Bit, SP, IR::OpSizeToSize(Size)));
 }
 
 void OpDispatchBuilder::POPAOp(OpcodeArgs) {
